  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday morning through next Sunday)\nIssued at 244 PM EDT Mon Sep 7 2026\n\nBeginning midweek, what remains of a secondary lobe of mid-level \nhigh pressure will be gradually nudged offshore by the first in a \nseries of shortwaves, accompanied by an advancing cold front. As \nsuch, most will remain dry on Wednesday (due to the continued \nimpacts of wedging at the surface), with chances for isolated to \nscattered showers and thunderstorms relegated to the southern third \nof the forecast area -- in the vicinity of a tendril of moisture \nextending across the northern Gulf. \n\nBy Thursday, with further breakdown of our residual ridging, rain \nchances will return in earnest areawide, lingering well into the \nweekend. Greatest coverage is expected Friday through Sunday, as the \naforementioned front breaches Georgia from the northwest, filtering \nsouthward and stalling out across the midsection of the state. \nCurrent forecast QPF over that 72-hour timeframe is manageable by \nFFG standards (up to an inch and a quarter), but care will need to \nbe taken to monitor for chances for localized flash flooding in \nareas that receive multiple rounds of heavy rainfall. \n\nThe weekend frontal passage looks to have minimal (if any) impacts \non our temperatures, and highs are expected to be in the upper 80s \nto lower 90s effectively each day in the extended. Morning lows will \nremain quite warm -- in the upper 60s to lower 70s, as much as 6 to \n12 degrees above average for this time of year.",
